WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.543
This is the technical deep dive
for Book 13 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:08.324
Modernization Workbook,
Governance, Drift and Evidence.

4
00:00:08.324 --> 00:00:12.082
It's for the people who keep
governance true after the audit:

5
00:00:12.082 --> 00:00:15.225
governance engineers,
platform owners and ISSOs.

6
00:00:15.225 --> 00:00:17.587
The whole book comes down to one idea.

7
00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:22.216
A package that's assembled by hand
starts going stale the day it's signed,

8
00:00:22.216 --> 00:00:27.237
so the evidence has to keep arriving, from
one record, in a form anyone can re-run.

9
00:00:27.237 --> 00:00:30.501
Part 1 builds the record and
the engine that watches it.

10
00:00:30.501 --> 00:00:35.037
Part 2 is the evidence, the owners,
and how authorization uses it.

11
00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:37.620
Let's start with why governance decays.

12
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.932
On the day a package is signed, it's true.

13
00:00:43.932 --> 00:00:46.579
The next morning someone fixes an outage,

14
00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.782
a role ends but the account stays
on, a provider ships a new default.

15
00:00:50.782 --> 00:00:52.558
None of it reaches the package.

16
00:00:52.558 --> 00:00:57.115
The program sees four structural reasons
this keeps happening, and none of them

17
00:00:57.115 --> 00:01:01.396
is fixed by buying another tool, because
each fix sits underneath the tools.

18
00:01:01.396 --> 00:01:06.195
Federal policy already points the other
way, toward ongoing authorization.

19
00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:09.715
What's missing is practice,
and NIST is blunt about why:

20
00:01:09.715 --> 00:01:12.595
without real automation,
you can't get there.

21
00:01:12.595 --> 00:01:14.920
So let's name the thing
that does the damage.

22
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.370
That's drift.

23
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.232
Module 1 is the foundation.

24
00:01:22.232 --> 00:01:24.929
You can only measure
drift against something,

25
00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:29.683
and that something only stays trustworthy
if every change goes through one path.

26
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:33.749
So we'll cover what drift is, the
one record we measure it against,

27
00:01:33.749 --> 00:01:36.450
and the path every change has to take.

28
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.913
Drift is any gap between what the record
says and what the estate actually does.

29
00:01:44.913 --> 00:01:49.759
NIST never uses the word, but its
configuration controls, read together,

30
00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:54.851
describe exactly this: a baseline,
controlled change, and someone checking.

31
00:01:54.851 --> 00:01:58.500
Drift comes from five places,
and most of them are innocent.

32
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.950
An emergency fix nobody wrote back.

33
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.863
A provider changing a default.

34
00:02:02.863 --> 00:02:04.074
People moving on.

35
00:02:04.074 --> 00:02:06.325
The dangerous part is that it's silent.

36
00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:09.031
Every system keeps
reporting what it was told,

37
00:02:09.031 --> 00:02:11.737
so nothing alarms unless
something compares.

38
00:02:11.737 --> 00:02:15.170
And a configuration that was
right at assessment and wrong

39
00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.462
six months later is exactly
what attackers walk through.

40
00:02:18.462 --> 00:02:20.990
Next, how the program sorts it.

41
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.335
If you call everything
configuration drift,

42
00:02:27.335 --> 00:02:29.790
you lose the ability to respond correctly.

43
00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:35.179
So the program sorts every finding into one
of five classes, each a simple question:

44
00:02:35.179 --> 00:02:37.873
is the shape valid, is
the value still true,

45
00:02:37.873 --> 00:02:40.884
does the change trace to
an authorized source,

46
00:02:40.884 --> 00:02:46.352
did anything act outside its authority, and
does every object resolve to the record.

47
00:02:46.352 --> 00:02:48.478
The payoff is in the response.

48
00:02:48.478 --> 00:02:52.278
The first two get fixed, at
the source, never in the copy.

49
00:02:52.278 --> 00:02:55.501
The last three might mean
someone acted without authority,

50
00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:58.002
so you investigate before
touching anything.

51
00:02:58.002 --> 00:03:01.710
Now, the record all of
this is measured against.

52
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.415
For years the directory tree
quietly was the governance model,

53
00:03:09.415 --> 00:03:10.860
and migrations lose it.

54
00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.783
The program's answer is one authoritative
record, kept in a governed repository,

55
00:03:15.783 --> 00:03:20.035
where every other copy is just a
pointer that knows where it came from.

56
00:03:20.035 --> 00:03:25.285
Single means logically single; replicas
are fine if only one accepts writes.

57
00:03:25.285 --> 00:03:28.908
Code follows three simple
rules: look it up by reference,

58
00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:32.610
never write to it at runtime,
and never keep a private copy.

59
00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.422
That last one is where
most real drift starts.

60
00:03:35.422 --> 00:03:37.980
And every report carries
a provenance block,

61
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:41.022
so you can always ask what
the value was on a given date.

62
00:03:41.022 --> 00:03:43.700
Next, how the record is divided.

63
00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:52.926
The record covers six control planes, from
identity at the root through addressing,

64
00:03:52.926 --> 00:03:55.862
network, telemetry,
security and management.

65
00:03:55.862 --> 00:03:59.626
They're cut by authority, by
who's allowed to say what's true,

66
00:03:59.626 --> 00:04:02.588
so every finding has
exactly one owning team.

67
00:04:02.588 --> 00:04:05.363
And the record doesn't master every fact.

68
00:04:05.363 --> 00:04:07.462
HR still owns who a worker is.

69
00:04:07.462 --> 00:04:10.303
What the record holds
is the authority map:

70
00:04:10.303 --> 00:04:13.225
who owns each fact, and
who may write the copy.

71
00:04:13.225 --> 00:04:14.475
Two rules follow.

72
00:04:14.475 --> 00:04:16.850
Corrections always flow upstream.

73
00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:21.625
And where the platform says a value is
read-only, the record has to know that too.

74
00:04:21.625 --> 00:04:23.650
Now, how change gets in.

75
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.338
Most drift isn't malicious.

76
00:04:29.338 --> 00:04:33.738
It's a quick fix in the portal or a weekend
script with a standing admin account.

77
00:04:33.738 --> 00:04:37.415
So every change, whether
a move, add, change,

78
00:04:37.415 --> 00:04:41.525
deletion or reset, travels
the same five steps.

79
00:04:41.525 --> 00:04:45.983
It starts from a source of truth,
gets approved by the right authority,

80
00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:50.988
runs with just-in-time privilege, closes
with proof, and emits its own evidence.

81
00:04:50.988 --> 00:04:55.487
Skip a step and it isn't a change with
missing paperwork; it's a drift finding.

82
00:04:55.487 --> 00:04:59.149
Pre-approved lanes are allowed,
but they're recorded grants

83
00:04:59.149 --> 00:05:02.587
with a scope and an expiry,
never the absence of approval.

84
00:05:02.587 --> 00:05:06.270
Next, the same path for
changing the record itself.

85
00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.082
When the record itself changes,
the pull request is the approval.

86
00:05:14.082 --> 00:05:19.103
It updates the registry, carries a
decision record for anything doctrinal,

87
00:05:19.103 --> 00:05:22.505
runs its checks before merge,
needs a second person,

88
00:05:22.505 --> 00:05:25.582
and merges signed, so
rollback is just a revert.

89
00:05:25.582 --> 00:05:30.078
Most repository platforms can enforce
this, with one catch worth knowing:

90
00:05:30.078 --> 00:05:33.320
the rules often don't bind
administrators by default.

91
00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.320
Turn that on, or you've
built a standing exception.

92
00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.681
The identity that decides and the
identity that acts are kept apart,

93
00:05:40.681 --> 00:05:42.783
so neither can finish a job alone.

94
00:05:42.783 --> 00:05:45.607
And emergencies get a path, not a pass.

95
00:05:45.607 --> 00:05:47.490
Which brings us to closure.

96
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.577
Here's the strictest rule in the book.

97
00:05:53.577 --> 00:05:57.679
If someone clicks complete with
nothing machine-readable attached,

98
00:05:57.679 --> 00:05:59.190
that's prose, not evidence.

99
00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.828
Every closure carries
the check that proved it.

100
00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:05.310
A human-only closure is still
allowed, as an escape hatch,

101
00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.852
but it's flagged and counted separately,
never blended into your metrics.

102
00:06:09.852 --> 00:06:14.840
And every report is regenerated from the
closure stream, never compiled by hand.

103
00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.715
One practical note: some of the controls
this supports, like non-repudiation,

104
00:06:19.715 --> 00:06:24.590
are only required at the High baseline,
so at Moderate the signing is a choice.

105
00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.440
That closes Module 1.

106
00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.510
Next, how we make the evidence checkable.

107
00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:36.198
Module 2 asks the obvious next question.

108
00:06:36.198 --> 00:06:40.259
When the program turns the record
into a statement about the estate,

109
00:06:40.259 --> 00:06:42.072
how does anyone know it's right?

110
00:06:42.072 --> 00:06:43.933
The answer is determinism,

111
00:06:43.933 --> 00:06:48.510
and the engine that uses it to
find, classify and record drift.

112
00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:55.097
Automation makes the answer arrive faster.

113
00:06:55.097 --> 00:06:58.519
Determinism makes it the same
answer no matter who runs it,

114
00:06:58.519 --> 00:07:03.010
and that's the only kind an assessor can
check without taking your word for it.

115
00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:07.797
Same record version, same inputs,
same evidence, byte for byte.

116
00:07:07.797 --> 00:07:12.846
That sounds easy and isn't: two runs can
agree and still serialize differently,

117
00:07:12.846 --> 00:07:16.710
so you need a fixed canonical
format and a pinned toolchain.

118
00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:18.885
The payoff is the picture on the left.

119
00:07:18.885 --> 00:07:22.478
The assessor re-runs the
window, and if the hashes match,

120
00:07:22.478 --> 00:07:25.273
the evidence is verified by reproduction.

121
00:07:25.273 --> 00:07:27.872
If they don't, that's a finding in itself.

122
00:07:27.872 --> 00:07:29.970
Now, where the rules live.

123
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.920
The rules live in three layers, and
keeping them apart is the point.

124
00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.833
The first says what a rule means.

125
00:07:39.833 --> 00:07:41.732
The second says where it applies.

126
00:07:41.732 --> 00:07:44.132
The third is the code that evaluates it.

127
00:07:44.132 --> 00:07:47.220
Each has its own reviewer
and leaves its own evidence.

128
00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.482
Why not combine them?

129
00:07:48.482 --> 00:07:52.340
Because they change at different
speeds, carry different blast radius,

130
00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.198
and when something drifts you want
to know whether the meaning shifted,

131
00:07:56.198 --> 00:07:58.945
a mapping broke, or the code
disagrees with the rule.

132
00:07:58.945 --> 00:08:02.358
And only the runtime is ever
allowed to change anything.

133
00:08:02.358 --> 00:08:05.820
Next, what rules as code
looks like in practice.

134
00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.858
Rules as code isn't a new idea.

135
00:08:11.858 --> 00:08:16.035
CISA's own secure-configuration
tool already works this way:

136
00:08:16.035 --> 00:08:21.032
read the tenant, compare against policy
written as code, report the result.

137
00:08:21.032 --> 00:08:24.261
Collection, decision and
reporting stay separate,

138
00:08:24.261 --> 00:08:26.644
and the policy is a file you can review.

139
00:08:26.644 --> 00:08:30.364
The key property is that evaluation
depends only on the rule,

140
00:08:30.364 --> 00:08:32.595
the mapping and the collected input.

141
00:08:32.595 --> 00:08:34.783
Never on who ran it, or when.

142
00:08:34.783 --> 00:08:39.213
On the right, the program lines its
artifacts up with NIST's machine-readable

143
00:08:39.213 --> 00:08:43.995
package models, so what it produces drops
straight into an authorization package.

144
00:08:43.995 --> 00:08:46.570
That correspondence is the program's own.

145
00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.720
Now, the engine itself.

146
00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:56.384
The drift engine finds the gap
between the record and the estate,

147
00:08:56.384 --> 00:08:58.895
classifies it and records what happened.

148
00:08:58.895 --> 00:09:01.972
The crucial choice is
what it compares against:

149
00:09:01.972 --> 00:09:05.207
a versioned record, not a
last-known-good snapshot.

150
00:09:05.207 --> 00:09:06.857
That gives you three things.

151
00:09:06.857 --> 00:09:09.146
You can reproduce any finding later.

152
00:09:09.146 --> 00:09:14.057
Approved changes don't show up as drift,
because they update the record first.

153
00:09:14.057 --> 00:09:17.139
And when the estate is right
and the record is stale,

154
00:09:17.139 --> 00:09:21.469
the engine still raises it, but the
fix is a reviewed change to the record.

155
00:09:21.469 --> 00:09:24.649
It checks on two clocks:
every change to the record,

156
00:09:24.649 --> 00:09:26.820
and scheduled reads of the estate.

157
00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.250
Next, one model for all of it.

158
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.886
Every earlier book defined
its own drift checks,

159
00:09:36.886 --> 00:09:39.800
for identity, delegation, HR and so on.

160
00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:40.963
They don't compete.

161
00:09:40.963 --> 00:09:43.957
Each one is an instance
of the same five classes,

162
00:09:43.957 --> 00:09:47.412
so a finding means the same
thing wherever it came from.

163
00:09:47.412 --> 00:09:52.413
One finding is worth calling out: a write
to a copy that was supposed to be retired.

164
00:09:52.413 --> 00:09:55.899
That's the sign a second source
of truth is growing back.

165
00:09:55.899 --> 00:09:58.438
And be honest with your
assessor about maturity.

166
00:09:58.438 --> 00:10:00.976
The model is complete; the engine isn't.

167
00:10:00.976 --> 00:10:04.312
Some classes are built, and
some are still found by people.

168
00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:06.113
Record those in the same shape.

169
00:10:06.113 --> 00:10:07.830
Now, severity.

170
00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.026
Severity isn't debated; it's looked up,

171
00:10:14.026 --> 00:10:17.043
from the class and whether
the claim is in use.

172
00:10:17.043 --> 00:10:21.018
So two people looking at the same
finding always get the same answer.

173
00:10:21.018 --> 00:10:25.505
But here's the most common design
mistake: treating severity as permission.

174
00:10:25.505 --> 00:10:27.317
Severity sets the clock.

175
00:10:27.317 --> 00:10:29.767
The actuation ladder sets who may act.

176
00:10:29.767 --> 00:10:34.073
A low-severity finding still waits
for a person unless its class has been

177
00:10:34.073 --> 00:10:38.593
explicitly approved to run on its own,
and some kinds of change never qualify.

178
00:10:38.593 --> 00:10:43.342
On the right, five things the engine
never does, including accepting risk.

179
00:10:43.342 --> 00:10:44.455
That ends Part 1.

180
00:10:44.455 --> 00:10:47.230
Next, what all this evidence is worth.

181
00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:52.692
Part 2 starts with evidence.

182
00:10:52.692 --> 00:10:56.823
We'll look at why system-produced
evidence beats a signed statement,

183
00:10:56.823 --> 00:10:59.626
exactly what the integrity
mechanisms prove,

184
00:10:59.626 --> 00:11:03.520
and the adapters that read
every platform into one record.

185
00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.495
A signature tells you who's accountable.

186
00:11:09.495 --> 00:11:13.470
It can't tell you whether the claim is
still true, and you can't re-run it.

187
00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.557
Evidence can.

188
00:11:14.557 --> 00:11:20.106
The program flips the usual package: evidence
becomes the substance, and people sign

189
00:11:20.106 --> 00:11:25.258
for the things machines can't decide, like
rules, exceptions and accepting risk.

190
00:11:25.258 --> 00:11:27.533
This isn't a stretch of federal guidance.

191
00:11:27.533 --> 00:11:32.138
NIST already says automated comparison
against a desired state counts as

192
00:11:32.138 --> 00:11:36.819
assessment results, and FedRAMP tells
assessors not to rely on screenshots.

193
00:11:36.819 --> 00:11:40.407
Some controls stay human, and
the package should say which.

194
00:11:40.407 --> 00:11:43.570
Next, the chain that
produces the evidence.

195
00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:51.247
The evidence chain has four
stages: capture a platform's state,

196
00:11:51.247 --> 00:11:55.635
normalize it into a common claim, seal
a window of claims into a bundle,

197
00:11:55.635 --> 00:12:00.332
and emit the package artifacts from that
one bundle, so they can't disagree.

198
00:12:00.332 --> 00:12:02.832
Every claim carries a provenance envelope.

199
00:12:02.832 --> 00:12:07.283
The fields that matter most say whether
a value was read from its owner,

200
00:12:07.283 --> 00:12:11.580
derived or made up by the program,
record every transformation step,

201
00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.882
and tie the claim to its exact source.

202
00:12:13.882 --> 00:12:18.683
One subtle point: a certificate
thumbprint only identifies a certificate.

203
00:12:18.683 --> 00:12:20.221
It doesn't sign anything.

204
00:12:20.221 --> 00:12:22.595
And drift findings ride the same chain.

205
00:12:22.595 --> 00:12:25.680
Next, what each integrity check proves.

206
00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:33.568
Integrity claims are easy
to oversell, so be precise.

207
00:12:33.568 --> 00:12:35.580
A hash tells you something changed,

208
00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.355
but anyone who changed
the data can recompute it.

209
00:12:38.355 --> 00:12:41.805
A hash chain tells you nothing
was removed or reordered.

210
00:12:41.805 --> 00:12:44.155
Only a signature tells
you who produced it.

211
00:12:44.155 --> 00:12:48.653
Together they let an assessor do
something an interview never can:

212
00:12:48.653 --> 00:12:50.780
re-run a past window and compare.

213
00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:52.693
And here's the practical advice.

214
00:12:52.693 --> 00:12:56.614
The re-run only counts as
independent if the assessor does it,

215
00:12:56.614 --> 00:13:01.792
not the operator, so give assessors their
own read access to the archived inputs.

216
00:13:01.792 --> 00:13:04.720
Now, the adapters that feed all this.

217
00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:11.120
The program doesn't
replace your platforms.

218
00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:12.120
It reads them.

219
00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:16.618
An adapter translates one platform's
native state into the common record,

220
00:13:16.618 --> 00:13:20.895
so the rules and the drift engine
never have to learn a vendor's dialect.

221
00:13:20.895 --> 00:13:24.157
Adapters are deliberately
narrow: no rules,

222
00:13:24.157 --> 00:13:27.057
no decisions, and no edits to the record.

223
00:13:27.057 --> 00:13:28.394
There are two kinds.

224
00:13:28.394 --> 00:13:30.245
Read-only adapters observe.

225
00:13:30.245 --> 00:13:34.245
Change-making adapters act,
but only on an authorized plan.

226
00:13:34.245 --> 00:13:36.760
They're kept in separate lists on purpose,

227
00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:41.345
so the grant of write authority is
visible in any change a reviewer looks at.

228
00:13:41.345 --> 00:13:44.770
Next, the contract every adapter keeps.

229
00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:52.111
An adapter is defined by its
declaration, not its code,

230
00:13:52.111 --> 00:13:56.518
and every one inherits four
invariants: stay inside the boundary,

231
00:13:56.518 --> 00:14:01.252
never edit the record, emit under
its own certificate-backed identity,

232
00:14:01.252 --> 00:14:03.782
and refer to real object identifiers.

233
00:14:03.782 --> 00:14:06.982
Read-only comes first,
but it has to be verified.

234
00:14:06.982 --> 00:14:11.383
A tool built to observe can still run
under an account that can change things,

235
00:14:11.383 --> 00:14:14.945
so compare what the identity is
granted with what it declares.

236
00:14:14.945 --> 00:14:17.842
Adapters also pin the
platform's production API,

237
00:14:17.842 --> 00:14:21.970
and earn active status through
testing before anyone relies on them.

238
00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.833
That closes Module 3.

239
00:14:23.833 --> 00:14:25.590
Next, the people.

240
00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:31.340
The machine can't decide anything.

241
00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:35.165
A drift finding is a question,
and someone has to own the answer.

242
00:14:35.165 --> 00:14:38.777
So Module 4 names the owners,
then shows how one body of

243
00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:43.490
evidence serves many agencies
and scales across a whole estate.

244
00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.052
Governance decays the
same way compliance does.

245
00:14:50.052 --> 00:14:52.677
If nobody owns a rule, it goes stale.

246
00:14:52.677 --> 00:14:56.177
If nobody signs for an
exception, it becomes permanent.

247
00:14:56.177 --> 00:14:58.816
So each question gets exactly one owner.

248
00:14:58.816 --> 00:15:03.848
Two jobs that are easy to blur are kept
apart: the steward keeps the record,

249
00:15:03.848 --> 00:15:08.965
but the information owners, like HR or
the CISO, decide what the facts mean.

250
00:15:08.965 --> 00:15:11.547
A steward who edits a
fact to make a finding

251
00:15:11.547 --> 00:15:14.202
disappear has made
someone else's decision.

252
00:15:14.202 --> 00:15:19.372
On the right, who decides each kind of rule
change, including the one that matters

253
00:15:19.372 --> 00:15:24.464
most: only the authorizing official lets
a class of change run without a person.

254
00:15:24.464 --> 00:15:26.350
Next, exceptions.

255
00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.437
A finding you can't fix in
time isn't quietly closed.

256
00:15:33.437 --> 00:15:36.888
It's carried, by a named
person, with an end date.

257
00:15:36.888 --> 00:15:39.423
A bounded exception is the CISO's call;

258
00:15:39.423 --> 00:15:42.988
anything open-ended goes to
the authorizing official.

259
00:15:42.988 --> 00:15:44.980
Nothing renews automatically,

260
00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:48.888
and an exception never edits the
rule, so the gap stays visible.

261
00:15:48.888 --> 00:15:51.881
The governance board reviews,
but it doesn't vote,

262
00:15:51.881 --> 00:15:54.575
because a consensus
has no one accountable.

263
00:15:54.575 --> 00:15:58.913
And because this platform holds the
record of what everyone else did,

264
00:15:58.913 --> 00:16:02.034
its own separations matter
most: no self-approval,

265
00:16:02.034 --> 00:16:06.600
assessors who never edit, and an
authorizing official who stays outside.

266
00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:09.280
Next, the tempo, in brief.

267
00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:16.292
A platform that detects
perfectly but closes slowly

268
00:16:16.292 --> 00:16:19.480
just builds an accurate list
of things nobody fixed.

269
00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:23.604
And sending a person to a console
because a ticket said so doesn't help;

270
00:16:23.604 --> 00:16:25.630
the closure is still an assertion.

271
00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:29.779
So wherever a machine can reach,
the ticket authorizes the machine,

272
00:16:29.779 --> 00:16:32.117
and the person's job stops at approval.

273
00:16:32.117 --> 00:16:35.642
A class of change qualifies
only if it passes six tests,

274
00:16:35.642 --> 00:16:39.324
and where no machine can reach,
the fallback is a recorded,

275
00:16:39.324 --> 00:16:42.692
time-boxed session, never a
quiet trip to the console.

276
00:16:42.692 --> 00:16:45.217
The reference deck walks
through the details.

277
00:16:45.217 --> 00:16:48.560
Now, reusing evidence across agencies.

278
00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.949
Twenty agencies assessing
the same platform

279
00:16:54.949 --> 00:16:57.797
produce twenty snapshots
that are all out of date.

280
00:16:57.797 --> 00:17:02.959
Reciprocity fixes the duplication, but it's
worth being precise about what it means.

281
00:17:02.959 --> 00:17:06.623
It's about reusing assessments,
not handing off risk.

282
00:17:06.623 --> 00:17:10.537
Federal policy now presumes a
maintained package is adequate,

283
00:17:10.537 --> 00:17:13.473
and asks for more only
with a documented reason.

284
00:17:13.473 --> 00:17:16.422
But every agency still
signs for its own system.

285
00:17:16.422 --> 00:17:21.316
So the program's model is one package for
a shared service, with each agency's own

286
00:17:21.316 --> 00:17:26.435
decision on top, and agencies differing
in configuration rather than in packages.

287
00:17:26.435 --> 00:17:29.420
Next, how that stays current over time.

288
00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:36.782
Reciprocity reuses one
package across agencies.

289
00:17:36.782 --> 00:17:42.296
Ongoing authorization reuses it across
time, and that's what makes reuse safe.

290
00:17:42.296 --> 00:17:46.549
Once the conditions are met, the
authorizing official stops working to

291
00:17:46.549 --> 00:17:50.658
a termination date and reviews
posture on a schedule and on triggers.

292
00:17:50.658 --> 00:17:53.604
Everything in this book is
what a robust monitoring

293
00:17:53.604 --> 00:17:56.070
program looks like when
it's actually built.

294
00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:57.233
Two cautions.

295
00:17:57.233 --> 00:18:02.220
FedRAMP's new security indicators
bind cloud providers, not agencies.

296
00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.600
And proving who produced a
bundle takes a real signature,

297
00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:09.195
because a shared-key code or a
list of hashes can be rewritten.

298
00:18:09.195 --> 00:18:11.360
Now, running this at scale.

299
00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:19.422
A real agency has hundreds of
systems and several programs at once.

300
00:18:19.422 --> 00:18:24.622
The scaling rule is simple: keep the
definitions small, and let the uses grow.

301
00:18:24.622 --> 00:18:29.672
If every program could invent its own
drift class, nobody's numbers would add up.

302
00:18:29.672 --> 00:18:34.548
Boundaries are treated the same way, as
recorded objects rather than assumptions.

303
00:18:34.548 --> 00:18:39.972
And continuous monitoring is run as an
actual chartered project, in five phases,

304
00:18:39.972 --> 00:18:44.986
each with a clear gate, starting with
just two sources and one object class.

305
00:18:44.986 --> 00:18:48.060
The risk to plan for is
finding more than you can fix.

306
00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.120
Next, how to report coverage honestly.

307
00:18:54.870 --> 00:19:00.008
No platform covers every control, and
pretending it does just hides risk.

308
00:19:00.008 --> 00:19:05.646
So every control gets an honest label:
covered, partial, gap or out of scope.

309
00:19:05.646 --> 00:19:09.107
Partial is the one most
often rounded up, so don't.

310
00:19:09.107 --> 00:19:12.800
Every gap goes in a register
with an owner and, crucially,

311
00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:16.332
the evidence that will prove
it closed, named up front.

312
00:19:16.332 --> 00:19:20.148
A gap closes one of three ways:
fix it, compensate for it,

313
00:19:20.148 --> 00:19:23.270
or have the authorizing
official accept it.

314
00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:27.363
And report counts, proven and
asserted, not one tidy percentage,

315
00:19:27.363 --> 00:19:30.983
so the decision maker can see
the risk they're accepting.

316
00:19:30.983 --> 00:19:32.340
Let's wrap up.

317
00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:38.715
So here are the decisions,
each with an owner.

318
00:19:38.715 --> 00:19:40.990
Charter the board and name the steward.

319
00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:45.165
Ratify which sources are authoritative
and retire every other copy.

320
00:19:45.165 --> 00:19:47.953
Make branch protection
bind administrators.

321
00:19:47.953 --> 00:19:51.786
Let only the authorizing official
decide what runs without a person,

322
00:19:51.786 --> 00:19:53.939
and how independent the assessors are.

323
00:19:53.939 --> 00:19:55.752
And report coverage honestly.

324
00:19:55.752 --> 00:19:59.674
The first steps don't need a new
platform: find the private copies,

325
00:19:59.674 --> 00:20:02.043
count human-only closures separately,

326
00:20:02.043 --> 00:20:05.965
check what your adapters can really
do, and open the gap register.

327
00:20:05.965 --> 00:20:08.809
Governance that doesn't
decay is governance

328
00:20:08.809 --> 00:20:11.653
whose gaps are written
down, owned and proven.

329
00:20:11.653 --> 00:20:12.470
Thank you.
